            <ArticleTitle>Rectus sheath haematoma: a rare masquerader for abdominal pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-219976</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-219976</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Rectus sheath haematoma is a rare cause of abdominal pain. It can be easily confused for other causes of acute abdomen and may even lead to unnecessary laparotomies. Our patient has the rectus sheath haematoma because of violent coughing and on presentation had no obvious clinical sign pointing to the same. Diagnosis was made by a CT scan of the abdomen, and patient was treated conservatively. Rectus sheath haematomas are usually present on the posterior aspect of the rectus muscles and thus may not be clinically appreciable.</AbstractText>
